The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (cl->knows_txdone && !shmem_channel_free(smbox->shmem)) {
```
```c
scmi_rx_callback(smbox->cinfo, shmem_read_header(smbox->shmem), NULL);
```
Reason:
The first statement checks if the channel is still owned by the platform, which indicates that a late reply could be causing a spurious interrupt. The second statement processes the message header from shared memory without verifying if the channel is free, leading to a race condition where the new request overwrites the header of the late reply, causing confusion in the SCMI stack.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cl->knows_txdone && !shmem_channel_free(smbox->shmem)) {
    dev_warn(smbox->cinfo->dev, "Ignoring spurious A2P IRQ !\n");
    return;
}
```
Reason:
This added check ensures that if the channel is still owned by the platform, the spurious interrupt is ignored, preventing the SCMI stack from processing a message that may not correspond to the current transaction. This effectively addresses the race condition by ensuring that only valid interrupts are processed when the channel is free.

